      <div>
        <p class="profile-detail-region">City · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Herat</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 550 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">The precise date of foundation is unknown. The city is first recorded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> (550 to 330 BCE), when the district was called Haraiva. The 14th-century writer Hamdallah Mustawfi said that a chief named Herat, a follower of the hero Nariman, first founded it, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> rebuilt it after it had fallen to ruin.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">South of the Paropamisus Mountains, in the fertile valley of the Hari River, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, on the roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Achaemenid district of Haraiva; Alexander's Artacoana; the Ghurid great mosque of 1201; the two Mongol sieges; the Kartid dynasty; the Timurid capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a>; the Musalla Complex; the poet and statesman Mir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a>; the painter Behzad</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Herat is the capital of Herat Province and the third-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with an estimated 673,273 settled residents.</span></div>
        </div>
