    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Herat was first recorded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> as the chief town of the district of Haraiva, and it passed to Alexander, the Parthian and Sasanian empires, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, the Ghaznavids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, the Ghurids and the Khwarazm Empire.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> besieged it twice, in the second siege ending its life as a great city for a time, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a>, it became the capital of an empire and a centre of art and learning.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> it was ruled by Afghan tribes, was independent for a time in the 19th century, was attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> several times, and was joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1863.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1979 Herat had about 140,000 people, and today it has an estimated 673,273 settled residents.</p></aside>
    </section>

