    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 330 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> captures the Arian capital, Artacoana, and rebuilds the town and builds the citadel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">651</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> and Turks fight the Arab forces in the battle of Qohestan and are defeated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1201</span><span class="rf-text">Ghiyasuddin Ghori builds the great mosque of Herat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1222</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> take Herat after a second siege lasting seven months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1380</span><span class="rf-text">Timur takes Herat and soon afterwards ends the Kartid dynasty</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1469</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a> begins his rule of Herat, which lasts until 1506</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1716</span><span class="rf-text">The Abdali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> of the city revolt and found the Sadozai Sultanate of Herat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1863</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> conquers Herat under Dost Muhammad Khan</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Herat lies south of the Paropamisus Mountains in the fertile valley of the Hari River, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It is first recorded in ancient times, but the date of its foundation is unknown. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> (550 to 330 BCE) the surrounding district was known by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> name Haraiva, and classical sources called the region Areia, or Aria. The Zoroastrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> names the district Haroiva. The names of the district and of its chief town come from that of the local river, which was called Harayu in Old Iranian, a word that means "with velocity". Naming a region and its main town after the river was common in this part of the world.</p><p>The Achaemenid inscriptions list the district among the provinces, for example in the Behistun inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, dated to about 520 BCE, and men from it are shown in the reliefs at Naqsh-e <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, wearing Scythian-style dress with a tunic, trousers tucked into high boots and a twisted hood. In the 14th century Hamdallah Mustawfi wrote that a chief named Herat, one of the followers of the hero Nariman, first founded the city, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> rebuilt it after it had fallen to ruin, with walls 9,000 paces round. In late 330 BCE Alexander captured the Arian capital, which was called Artacoana. The town was rebuilt and the citadel was constructed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-rukh.jpg" alt="Shah Rukh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Shah_Rukh,_painted_in_his_lifetime_in_1435-36,_by_commission_of_his_son_Ibrahim_Sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1435-1436 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Herat was a trading centre on routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and geographers of the Abbasid period noted its textiles. In the 10th century Estakhri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> described a prosperous town with strong walls, many sources of water, extensive suburbs, an inner citadel, a congregational mosque and four gates, each opening on a market. The government building stood about a mile outside the city at Khorasanabad. A church was still visible in the countryside to the north-east, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temple</a> stood on a hilltop farther away.</p><p>Mustawfi wrote that Herat flourished especially under the Ghurids in the 12th century. He reported 359 colleges, 12,000 shops all occupied, 6,000 bath-houses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, mills, a dervish convent and a fire temple, and about 444,000 houses with a settled population. The great mosque was built by Ghiyasuddin Ghori in 1201, and the city was a centre for metal goods, especially bronze with inlays of precious metals.</p><p>The city reached its greatest glory under the Timurid princes, especially Sultan Husayn Bayqara, who ruled it from 1469 until 4 May 1506. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a>, the youngest son of Timur, had been named governor in 1397 and encouraged building, manufacturing and trade, restoring and enlarging the bazaar. The Musalla Complex, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of Gawhar Shad and many gardens date from this time. The period was one of relative stability, prosperity and cultural activity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar.jpg" alt="Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_illustrated_and_illuminated_folio_from_a_manuscript_of_Husayn_Va%E2%80%99iz_al-Kashifi%E2%80%99s_Akhlaq-i_Muhsini;_Ya%27qub_ibn_Layth_standing_on_the_roof_in_full_armour,_Persia,_Shiraz,_Safavid,_16th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sotheby's, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Herat was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and then fell to Alexander. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> in 167 BCE. In the Sasanian period (226 to 652 CE) it was one of the empire's provincial capitals, and in the last two centuries of Sasanian rule it was of great strategic importance in the wars between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, the Chionites and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>. The Arab armies appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in the 650s, and Herat submitted and received an Arab governor, though like many places in Khorasan it rebelled and was reconquered several times. By 659 to 661 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> claimed a tenuous suzerainty over it, until local Turkish tribes drove the Tang out in 665.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar</a> took Herat in the 860s and 870s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> expelled the Taherids from Khorasan in 873. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> ruled it for about a century, and their power was destroyed in 999 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Qarakhanids</a> and the Ghaznavids. Herat was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> in 1040, by the Ghurids in 1175 and by the Khwarazm Empire in 1214. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, a local prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-muhammad-kart">Shams al-Din Kart</a>, was named ruler in 1244 and confirmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> in 1255, and his Kartid dynasty ruled until Timur took the city in 1380.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> occupied Herat in 1507, and Shah Isma'il of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> took it in 1510. The Uzbeks held it again after his death until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp</a> retook it in 1528. Under the Safavids it was a provincial capital, and in the 16th century every Safavid ruler from Tahmasp I to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a> was governor of Herat in his youth. By the early 18th century the Abdali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> governed it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai.jpg" alt="Ali-Shir Nava'i" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mystics_in_a_garden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qasim Ali, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most sources suggest that Herat was predominantly Zoroastrian in the centuries before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Around 430 it had a Christian community with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, hastily converted peoples elsewhere returned to their old beliefs once the armies had passed, and Herat itself was the scene of conflicts between different groups of Muslims and Arab tribes in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and a centre of the followers of Ustadh Sis. In the 12th century, according to Mustawfi, the men were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims who carried arms.</p><p>Herat produced learned men, among them the 11th-century mystic and poet Khwajah Abdullah Ansari, whose tomb at the village of Gazar Gah was first rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> about 1425 and became a shrine with other famous men buried around it. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> the court was known for its sumptuous life, its music and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Mir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a>, a poet and author in Persian and Turkish, was a great builder and patron of the arts, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> and the painter Behzad also belonged to the city's history. The city had a Jewish community, with about 280 families in 1948, most of whom moved to Israel that year. The community had disappeared by 1992, and four former synagogues survive in the old quarter.</p><p>Most people in Herat speak Dari, a form of Persian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> is the second major language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> form the majority of the population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> besieged Herat twice. The first siege ended in the surrender of the city, the slaughter of the local sultan's army of 12,000, and the appointment of two governors, one Mongol and one Muslim. The second was prompted by a rebellion against Mongol rule and lasted seven months, ending in June 1222. According to one account, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> beheaded the entire population of 1,600,000, such that no head was left on a body and no body had a head. The city remained in ruins from 1222 to about 1236.</p><p>In 1793 Herat became independent for several years during the civil war among the sons of Timur Shah. The Iranians fought wars with Herat between 1801 and 1837, in 1804, 1807, 1811, 1814, 1817, 1818, 1821, 1822, 1825 and 1833, and besieged the city in 1837, when the British helped the Heratis repel them. In 1856 the Iranians invaded again and briefly took the city on 25 October, which led directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-war">Anglo-Persian War</a>. During the Panjdeh incident, the Musalla of Gawhar Shad was dynamited to keep the advancing Russian forces from using it.</p><p>In March 1979 the Afghan Army in Herat under Ismail Khan mutinied and protesters rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> government; the rebels held the city for three days. Reprisals followed, and between 3,000 and 24,000 people, according to different sources, were killed. In April 1983 Soviet bombings damaged half of the city and killed an estimated 3,000 civilians. The Taliban captured the city on 5 September 1995 without much resistance, and put down a rebellion there in May 1999.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara.jpg" alt="Husayn Bayqara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sultan_Husayn_Mirza_Bayqara_at_the_age_of_about_50_years._1500%E2%80%9325_copy_from_Behz%C4%81d%27s_original_of_about_1490.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Herat was called the pearl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> wrote of it as the pearl in the middle of the oyster of that province. After Timur's conquest it became an important centre of intellectual and artistic life in the Islamic world, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a> it was the focal point of the Timurid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, whose grandeur is thought to have matched that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. In Safavid texts it was called the greatest of the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and Shah Abbas the Great was born there.</p><p>The city holds the Great Mosque, built by Ghiyasuddin Ghori in 1201, the citadel, called Qala Ikhtyaruddin or the Arg, and the Musalla Complex. Of the more than a dozen minarets that once stood in the city, many have been toppled by war and neglect during the past century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> personnel and Afghan authorities have worked to stabilise the Fifth Minaret. The tomb of Dost Muhammad Khan, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a>, is in the city.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> conquered Herat on 26 May 1863 under Dost Muhammad Khan, two weeks before his death, and it has been part of Afghanistan since. Herat fell to the Taliban on 12 August 2021. It is the capital of Herat Province and the third-largest city of Afghanistan.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Herat, in the Hari River valley of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, was an Achaemenid district town, a Ghurid city and the seat of the Kartid dynasty, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> the capital of an empire and a centre of art. It joined Afghanistan in 1863 and is its third-largest city.</em></p>

